WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.780
Several pictures can all represent DNA.

2
00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:05.440
Which one should you trust?

3
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:08.540
The answer changes with your question.

4
00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:12.820
Keep one short sample in view while its pictures change.

5
00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:19.690
A scientific model is a simplified representation.

6
00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:23.450
It helps explain or predict something real.

7
00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:26.670
It is a tool, not a perfect copy.

8
00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:29.930
Every tool has a job and a limit.

9
00:00:30.983 --> 00:00:33.023
Ask which bases face each other.

10
00:00:34.223 --> 00:00:35.463
A flat ladder helps.

11
00:00:36.843 --> 00:00:40.183
Labelled base blocks form rungs between plain backbone bars.

12
00:00:41.583 --> 00:00:44.163
Pairing is clear, but the twist and atoms are gone.

13
00:00:45.233 --> 00:00:46.993
Ask for base order instead.

14
00:00:48.413 --> 00:00:50.553
The ladder folds into a straight letter strip.

15
00:00:51.893 --> 00:00:53.733
Comparing positions becomes easy.

16
00:00:55.333 --> 00:00:58.473
Shape, size, and the partner strand disappear from this view.

17
00:00:59.517 --> 00:01:03.677
A ribbon model answers a shape question by showing coiling strands.

18
00:01:05.057 --> 00:01:07.717
A space-filling model shows occupied space.

19
00:01:09.077 --> 00:01:11.177
Neither is best for reading the exact order.

20
00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:17.760
Now predict. You need to show which bases face each other across the strands.

21
00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:21.320
Which model should move under the question lens?

22
00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:23.660
Hold your answer.

23
00:01:29.433 --> 00:01:32.973
The ladder moves forward because its rungs expose pairing.

24
00:01:34.213 --> 00:01:37.633
The other models slide back, still useful for other questions.

25
00:01:39.133 --> 00:01:43.053
Choose DNA models by the question; every model leaves features out.

26
00:01:44.083 --> 00:01:45.923
Usefulness is not enough.

27
00:01:47.483 --> 00:01:48.603
Evidence can reject a model.

28
00:01:50.003 --> 00:01:54.843
Measured base amounts were close in pairs: adenine with thymine, and guanine with

29
00:01:54.843 --> 00:02:00.063
cytosine. A structure model had to explain that pattern, or be ruled out.

30
00:02:01.067 --> 00:02:05.047
X-ray diffraction added distances, width, and shape clues.

31
00:02:06.347 --> 00:02:08.607
It came from spots made by lined-up fibers.

32
00:02:09.947 --> 00:02:13.547
The pattern was indirect evidence, not a photograph of bases.

33
00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:16.940
Chemistry supplied another filter.

34
00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:21.360
Watson and Crick built a model fitting all the constraints.

35
00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:27.820
Backbones stayed outside, bases paired inside, and the strands ran in opposite

36
00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:28.820
directions.

37
00:02:29.583 --> 00:02:32.223
Pairing also suggested a copying idea.

38
00:02:33.503 --> 00:02:37.523
Separate the strands, and each row fixes the matching row beside it.

39
00:02:38.623 --> 00:02:41.723
That was a prediction the model made, not proof by itself.

40
00:02:42.867 --> 00:02:45.327
The result was the double helix model.

41
00:02:46.747 --> 00:02:48.867
Watson and Crick still called it unproved.

42
00:02:50.227 --> 00:02:52.807
Franklin and Gosling called a helix highly probable.

43
00:02:54.227 --> 00:02:56.387
Later tests supported its main features.

44
00:02:57.500 --> 00:02:59.460
Return to the original sample.

45
00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:04.460
Pairing, shape, order, and occupied space still need different views.

46
00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:09.120
Start with the question, then inspect what your chosen view hides.
